        "prompts": [
          {
            "after": null,
            "at_step": 1,
            "concept": "The reference panel shows the true tree this matrix came from: every distance in the cells is the exact path between two tips of that tree. A matrix like this is called additive — it holds the whole tree, if the method can find it. Start with Neighbor-Joining: build and step to the end.",
            "math": "On an additive matrix, d(i,j) equals the total branch length on the path from i to j — building the tree is exactly the inverse problem of reading the matrix.",
            "reveal": null,
            "reveal_pair": null,
            "type": "note"
          },
          {
            "after": null,
            "at_step": 5,
            "concept": "If you are on Neighbor-Joining, the tree is now complete and the comparison shows no differences — every split of the true tree came back, because NJ is built to invert additive distances. Then switch the Method control to UPGMA and step through to the end: watch whether its tree still matches.",
            "math": "NJ's Q score corrects for how far each cluster sits from all the others, so it copes with branches of very different lengths.",
            "reveal": null,
            "reveal_pair": null,
            "type": "note"
          },
          {
            "after": null,
            "at_step": 6,
            "concept": "With UPGMA, the comparison shows a difference: UPGMA joins the closest pair and halves the distance — exact only when every tip is the same distance from the root. This tree is not clock-like, so its cluster averages pull a wrong pair together.",
            "math": "Cluster averaging keeps the matrix consistent only under a molecular clock; unequal branch lengths break it, and the wrong join order follows.",
            "reveal": null,
            "reveal_pair": null,
            "type": "note"
          }
        ],
